#: docs/user-guide/concepts/behavior-systems.md:1
msgid "Behavior systems"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/behavior-systems.md:3
msgid "A **behavior system** is a design made of component **instances** wired together, alongside the system's own concepts, relationships, timing domains and physical outputs. Every project holds one; the simplest case is a system with no components — the lamp of the tutorials."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/behavior-systems.md:16
msgid "The whole thing — top level plus every instance's source — is one design to the checker, the simulator and the deployment check. There is no second language for systems."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/behavior-systems.md:20
msgid "Source and instance"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/behavior-systems.md:22
msgid "A **component** is a definition. An **instance** is one use of it, with a name (`lampA`, `lampB`), a place on the canvas, values for its parameters, and a domain of the system for each of its timing parameters. Two instances of one component compute the same thing over different inputs; edit the component's source and both change."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/behavior-systems.md:28
msgid "On the canvas an instance is a node drawn from the component's promise: one row per port — _requires_ and _parameter_ on the left, _provides_ on the right — and the component's name in the body. The node never shows the inside of the source; double-click it to open the source."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/behavior-systems.md:35
#: docs/user-guide/studio/system-projects.md:28
msgid "_Two instances of AdaptiveLamp, drawn from the component's promise, fed by one tiltValue and bound to brightness and mirror._"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/behavior-systems.md:38
msgid "Bindings"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/behavior-systems.md:40
msgid "A **binding** connects a provided port (or a top-level value) to a required port (or an open top-level relationship). It is drawn as a link between the instance nodes. A binding converts nothing: both ends carry the same concept, by identity, and the destination simply _is_ the source's value."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/behavior-systems.md:45
msgid "Connection"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/behavior-systems.md:45
msgid "Allowed when"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/behavior-systems.md:47
msgid "provided port → required port"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/behavior-systems.md:47
msgid "same concept; same domain, or an explicit transport"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/behavior-systems.md:48
msgid "top-level value → required port"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/behavior-systems.md:48
msgid "same concept; same domain, or transport"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/behavior-systems.md:49
msgid "provided port → open top-level relationship"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/behavior-systems.md:49
msgid "the relationship reads nothing and has no formula; it becomes _bound to_ the port and cannot be given a formula while bound"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/behavior-systems.md:51
msgid "**Fan-out** is ordinary: one provided port or one top-level value can feed any number of required ports. The value is computed once and read by each; nothing is duplicated."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/behavior-systems.md:55
msgid "A required port takes **one** source. Dropping a second link onto a bound port asks _Replace the connection?_ — _Disconnect and Connect_ or _Cancel_ — never a silent replacement."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/behavior-systems.md:59
msgid "Open inputs of a system"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/behavior-systems.md:61
msgid "A required port with no binding is **open**: _Open: nothing supplies it yet._ An open port is not an error; the instance's value for it is missing, exactly as a declared top-level relationship's is. On the Simulate page an open port appears as an input you can type, so a partly wired system can be simulated. A system is **executable** when every required port that something depends on is bound and every top-level rule holds."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/behavior-systems.md:68
msgid "Timing across instances"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/behavior-systems.md:70
msgid "A component's timing parameters are bound per instance to the system's domains. When a binding crosses domains — a provided port in _interaction_ feeding a required port in _display_ — the link asks for a _Starts at_ value and records a **transport**: the destination reads the source's last activation strictly before its own tick, and the initial value before then. This is the same rule as `sync` in a formula ([Timing](timing.md)); on the canvas the transport is a gate on the link."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/behavior-systems.md:78
msgid "Physical outputs and instances"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/behavior-systems.md:80
msgid "Outputs stay the system's. An instance can drive a system output through a provided port bound to the top-level value that drives it; two instances driving one output through two values is the same **contested** output as in a plain design, reported the same way. When you package a group, the packaging sheet asks whether each driven output _stays the system's_ or _moves inside_ the component."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/behavior-systems.md:87
msgid "Simulating and deploying a system"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/behavior-systems.md:89
msgid "Simulate and Deploy always work on the whole system. An instance's values appear as `lampA.brightness`; its open ports as inputs. Deployment counts every device the system's outputs need, whichever instance drives them."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/behavior-systems.md:93
msgid "What is not there yet"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/behavior-systems.md:95
msgid "Nested systems (a component whose source contains instances), packages shared between projects, and behavioral checks between component versions (a substitution is checked for its _wiring_, not for computing the same values) — see [Versioning a component](../workflows/component-versioning.md)."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/behavior-systems.md:102
msgid "_For language implementers._ The authored system is flattened — instantiate, freshen identities, substitute clocks, realise parameters, union with the base, realise bindings as `declRef` / `sync` references — into an ordinary flat design, and every judgment is a judgment of that flat design (ADR-0021 \"behaviour systems flatten into the flat design\"). Identities: `ComponentId` (a definition), `ComponentInstanceId` (one use), `PortId`, `BindingId`, and the flat `SemanticId` / `DeclId` / `ClockId` / `OutputId` per instance — five different kinds of identity that the tool never collapses. `docs/evidence/behavior-systems-correspondence.md` states which formal theorems each production step follows and which are restricted (the modular-equals-flat result is proved for a single-domain fragment; the production claims a differential test, not the theorem)."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/behavior-systems.md:117
msgid "[Components](components.md) · [System projects](../studio/system-projects.md) · [Workflow: composing components](../workflows/composing-components.md) · [Workflow: carrying a value across timing domains](../workflows/cross-domain-transport.md)"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/components.md:1
msgid "Components"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/components.md:3
msgid "A **component** is a reusable behavior with an explicit boundary. It has a **source** — an ordinary design of its own: concepts, relationships, timing, groups — and a **promise**: the ports through which the rest of a system uses it. A component is used by placing **instances** of it; every instance runs the same source, and editing the source changes every instance."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/components.md:9
msgid "A [behavior group](behavior-groups.md) is a way of seeing a design. A component is a _different thing_: a definition with a public boundary that the rest of the design can only reach through that boundary. Most groups never become components; a component is what you make when a behavior will be used more than once, or should be understood without looking inside."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/components.md:15
msgid "Components exist in every project ([System projects](../studio/system-projects.md))."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/components.md:18
msgid "The promise: ports"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/components.md:20
msgid "A port is a relationship of the source that the component exposes. There are three kinds:"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/components.md:23
msgid "Port"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/components.md:23
msgid "In designer words"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/components.md:23
msgid "In the source"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/components.md:25
msgid "**Requires**"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/components.md:25
msgid "what this behavior _needs_ from outside — a value it reads but does not produce"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/components.md:25
msgid "a relationship without inputs that is left **declared**; the instance's wiring supplies it"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/components.md:26
msgid "**Provides**"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/components.md:26
msgid "what this behavior _offers_ — a value others can use"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/components.md:26
msgid "a relationship without inputs that is **defined** inside the source"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/components.md:27
msgid "**Parameter**"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/components.md:27
msgid "what is _configured_ when the component is used — a constant per instance"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/components.md:27
msgid "a declared relationship without inputs, given a closed value (a number with a unit, `true`, …) on each instance"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/components.md:29
msgid "Each port carries a **contract**: the concept it produces, and how it relates to timing — _any domain_, _its own private domain_, or _a timing parameter of the component_ (a domain the instance chooses). The contract is stored with the port, not read off the source, so the component can promise something and the tool can tell you when the source **no longer keeps the promise**: the component's row gets a red mark and its instances are drawn _unrealized_ until the source or the contract is fixed."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/components.md:37
msgid "**Timing parameters** are the component's domains that each instance maps to a domain of the system. A component that updates _in its own_ domain brings that domain with it."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/components.md:41
msgid "Shared and private"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/components.md:43
msgid "Inside the source a concept is normally **private**: each instance gets its own _Brightness_, distinct from every other instance's and from the system's. A component can instead declare a concept **shared** — _this is the system's Brightness_ — so that its ports carry the same concept as the rest of the design. Sharing is a decision you make; it is never inferred from names, units or library templates."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/components.md:50
msgid "Where components come from"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/components.md:52
msgid "**Packaging a behavior group** — the usual way. _Package as Reusable Component…_ on a group turns its members into a component's source, the group's boundary into ports, and leaves one instance where the group stood. [Workflow: packaging a behavior](../workflows/package-as-component.md)."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/components.md:56
msgid "**From scratch** — **+** by _Components_ in the sidebar, then _Edit Source_: an empty design. Select the component in the sidebar: its inspector's **Declare a port** section lists the source's relationships and lets you expose one as _requires_, _provides_ or _parameter_."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/components.md:61
msgid "Editing a component"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/components.md:63
msgid "Double-click an instance, or select the component and choose **Edit Source**. The canvas switches to the component's own design; the bar above it says _Editing AdaptiveLamp · used by 3 instances_ with a _‹ System_ link back. Everything on the Design page works here as in a plain design — sheets, formula editor, groups, completion, hover in the formula field — in the component's own names. What you change reaches every instance when you save the definition."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/components.md:70
msgid "The component's inspector (select it in the sidebar) shows its **Ports** with their contracts, **Timing parameters**, **Shared concepts**, and the instances that use it, and offers **Duplicate as Version**, port retirement, and deletion (_Delete its instances first._)."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/components.md:75
msgid "What a component is not"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/components.md:77
msgid "Not a group with a different icon: a group's box sockets are a picture; a component's ports are a promise other things depend on."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/components.md:79
msgid "Not a copy: two instances share one source. To have two versions of a behavior, _Duplicate as Version_ ([Versioning a component](../workflows/component-versioning.md))."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/components.md:82
msgid "Not portable across projects yet: a component lives in the project that defines it."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/components.md:84
msgid "Not nestable yet: a component's source is a flat design; a behavior group inside a source can be made and used, but not packaged into a component of its own."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/components.md:90
msgid "_For language implementers._ A component is `BehaviorComponent { body: Design, interface, shared_concepts, external_outputs }`; its interface is a stored promise per port (`PortContract`), checked against the body by `Realizes` once per component, not per instance (ADR-0022). Instances are freshened copies with private identities; bindings are references by identity, never by name. `docs/evidence/behavior-systems-correspondence.md` maps every production object to the formal development; `docs/architecture/behavior-systems.md` is the implementation design."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/components.md:101
msgid "[Behavior groups](behavior-groups.md) · [Behavior systems](behavior-systems.md) · [System projects](../studio/system-projects.md) · [Workflow: composing components](../workflows/composing-components.md)"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/concepts.md:1
#: docs/user-guide/textual/syntax-basics.md:32
msgid "Concepts"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/concepts.md:3
msgid "A **concept** is something the product senses, decides or shows, given a name: _Tilt_, _Brightness_, _Held_, _Battery level_. A concept is the unit of meaning in a BDL design. Relationships read concepts and produce concepts; outputs accept a concept; everything else is built from them."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/concepts.md:8
msgid "A concept is a meaning, not a number"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/concepts.md:10
msgid "_Brightness_ and _Opacity_ may both be plain numbers between 0 and 1. _Battery level_ may be a plain number between 0 and 1 too. They are three concepts, and BDL keeps them apart: a relationship that produces _Opacity_ cannot be connected where _Brightness_ is read, however the numbers happen to line up. This is what makes a design checkable — the tool can tell you that you have wired a level into a brightness, which no spreadsheet ever will."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/concepts.md:17
msgid "On the canvas this shows as colour. Every concept has its own hue, and a link can only join sockets of the same hue. While you drag a link, the sockets that can accept it light up; the others do not."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/concepts.md:21
#: docs/user-guide/concepts/concepts.md:25
msgid "Value form"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/concepts.md:23
msgid "A concept has a **value form** — what kind of value it carries:"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/concepts.md:25
msgid "Socket"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/concepts.md:25
msgid "Examples"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/concepts.md:27
msgid "**Quantity** — a measured amount, with a unit"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/concepts.md:27
msgid "○ round"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/concepts.md:27
msgid "an angle in `rad`, a length in `m`, a temperature in `K`; or _no unit_ for a plain level"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/concepts.md:28
msgid "**On / off** — a truth value"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/concepts.md:28
msgid "◇ diamond"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/concepts.md:28
msgid "_Held_, _Button pressed_, _Door open_"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/concepts.md:29
msgid "**Count** — a whole number"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/concepts.md:29
msgid "□ square"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/concepts.md:29
msgid "_Steps_, _Items_, _Presses_"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/concepts.md:30
msgid "**Decide later** — not chosen yet"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/concepts.md:30
msgid "hollow ring"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/concepts.md:30
msgid "any concept you have named but not pinned down"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/concepts.md:32
msgid "For a quantity the unit is chosen from a list of quantity kinds (angle, length, time, mass, temperature, current, and derived kinds such as speed, frequency, voltage, illuminance). The symbol is shown beside the kind. The choice fixes the concept's _dimension_, which is what formulas are checked against: a _Tilt_ in radians plus a _Time_ in seconds is refused; a _Tilt_ divided by `90 deg` is a plain number."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/concepts.md:39
msgid "A quantity with _no unit_ is still a quantity. Brightness as a level from 0 to 1 is the usual example."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/concepts.md:42
msgid "Decide later"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/concepts.md:44
msgid "You can name a concept before you know how it is measured. Its sockets are drawn as hollow rings, relationships can already read and produce it, and any formula over it waits: the inspector says _Checked once Temperature's value is decided._ Nothing is wrong. See [Incomplete designs](incomplete-designs.md)."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/concepts.md:49
msgid "Choosing a value form the first time is a refinement — nothing that depends on the concept needs rechecking. _Changing_ a value form that was already chosen is an edit, and the inspector names the relationships that will be checked again."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/concepts.md:53
msgid "Where concepts come from"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/concepts.md:55
msgid "**The _New concept_ sheet** (sidebar → _Project_ → **+** by _Concepts_): name, value form, unit, meaning. The sheet previews the row as you type."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/concepts.md:57
msgid "**The Library tab**: ready-made concepts — _Temperature_, _Tilt_, _Button Pressed_, _Motor Speed_, … — grouped by role. Drag one onto the canvas or use the canvas's right-click **Add Concept** menu. The result is an ordinary concept with a suggested name and value form; two insertions of the same template are two concepts. See [Library](../studio/library.md)."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/concepts.md:63
#: docs/user-guide/concepts/relationships.md:98
msgid "What the inspector shows"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/concepts.md:65
msgid "**Meaning** — the name and a free-text description of what the concept means to the product."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/concepts.md:67
msgid "**Value** — the value form and unit."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/concepts.md:68
msgid "**Relationships** — _Produced by_ and _Used by_, as links."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/concepts.md:69
msgid "**Delete** — disabled while a relationship uses the concept, with the users named."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/concepts.md:74
msgid "_For language implementers._ A concept is a nominal semantic type; its value form is its _representation_, chosen once (a write-once binding). A formula reads a concept through `rep` and produces one through `mk`, and the dimension check is the representation's dimension algebra. `docs/spec/kernel.md` and `docs/architecture/compiler-pipeline.md` (passes 3–7) are the reference; `docs/spec/concept-library.md` explains why a library template is not an identity."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/concepts.md:84
msgid "[Relationships](relationships.md) · [Incomplete designs](incomplete-designs.md) · [Library](../studio/library.md) · [Types, units and concepts](../troubleshooting/type-and-concept-errors.md)"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/incomplete-designs.md:1
msgid "Incomplete designs"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/incomplete-designs.md:3
msgid "In most tools an unfinished thing is an error. In BDL it is a state. You can say _the lamp needs a brightness behavior_ before you know the formula, _there will be a temperature_ before you know how it is measured, _this output exists_ before anything drives it — and save, simulate what is ready, and check the rest tomorrow."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/incomplete-designs.md:9
msgid "This page explains the states a design and its parts can be in, what each looks like, and which ones actually stop you."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/incomplete-designs.md:12
